{
  "image_name": "2b32527d97620f35eaed5040215045d0.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a contractile ring is assembled in fission yeast through a “search, capture, pull, and release” cycle. At the top, individual cortical nodes send out actin filaments. Nearby nodes capture these filaments when they come within a certain distance, and myosin II then pulls connected nodes toward one another. Because these links are temporary, the actin connections eventually break, allowing the cycle to repeat. This repeated local interaction appears to progressively draw scattered nodes together.\n\nThe lower part places this mechanism into the context of the whole cell cycle. During interphase, the cell contains dispersed Cdc12 speckles. As division begins, Cdc12 joins nodes near the cell middle, where actin filaments grow from these nodes. The filaments and nodes then organize into bundles, which coalesce into a continuous red contractile ring around the cell equator. Once complete, the ring constricts, the septum forms, and the cell separates into two daughters.\n\nOverall, the image shows a dynamic self-organization process: many transient node–actin interactions collectively assemble the stable cytokinetic ring that drives cell division.",
